    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Lawrence Butler** (66.43) and **Geraldo Perdomo** (65.08) headline the lineup as our most efficient starts. **Lawrence Butler** benefits immensely from a favorable matchup against Soriano, where his dynamic platoon advantage (+13%) and a park factor boost drive his elite-tier projection. Meanwhile, **Geraldo Perdomo**\u2019s high score is anchored by his spot in the #2 hole and a favorable matchup against Prielipp, where his contact profile translates well into high-floor production. **Tyler Freeman** is another highlight; despite the marginal concern regarding the opposing catcher\u2019s elite pop-time, his +14% park factor and stable order placement make him a premier play for LawDog today.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved **Drew Gilbert**, whose projection of 38.46 fell just shy of our 40.0 \"Floor\" threshold. Despite a decent base projection, his utility is crushed by a bottom-tier batting order spot (#8) and the severe SP skill disadvantage posed by Bachar, forcing us to keep him on the pine to preserve our limited game caps. We are also closely monitoring the pending status of **Jeremiah Jackson** and **Taylor Ward**. Both players are projected to start in the #8 and #1 spots respectively, and their scores are significantly propped up by the \"Wind Out\" factor at their venue. However, facing Roki Sasaki introduces substantial risk; while the potential for stolen bases from **Jeremiah Jackson**\u2014boosted by the pitcher\u2019s slow delivery\u2014keeps him in the lineup, we must remain cognizant that elite arms can render these analytical edges moot.\n\nFinally, while **Gavin Sheets** rounds out the lineup at 42.18, he represents a high-risk play. Facing deGrom is the primary concern, as the opposing SP skill penalty (-19%) is the harshest on the board today. We are playing him strictly for the platoon edge and lineup volume, but he remains our \"softest\" start. With **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** and **Daulton Varsho** still sidelined on the IL, we are stretched thin; the current configuration remains our best path to maximizing efficiency in SB and R, provided our outfielders capitalize on their park-driven advantages against the day's favorable pitching matchups.",
